WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.340
As the year continues to deliver a potent
slate of movies possessed of unholy terrors,

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.490
nefarious characters, and beastly delights,
we're happy to chronicle each and every one

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.240
of them for you.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.120
These are the most disturbing movies of 2019.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.580
Director and screenwriter Dan Gilroy last
teamed up with Jake Gyllenhaal for a caustic

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:23.199
media satire about an overly ambitious young
man trying to break into the gritty world

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.520
of crime journalism.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.080
Nightcrawler seamlessly blended biting satire
with a distinctly dark sensibility, even using

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.060
horror movie tropes to give the action a nightmarish,
post-modern quality.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.060
Featuring one of the best performances of
Gyllenhaal's career, Nightcrawler proved to

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.240
be one of 2014's best films.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.580
In fact, the gruesome, gritty film was such
a success, the pair teamed up once again for

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.740
Gilroy's latest satirical confection...the
supernatural thriller Velvet Buzzsaw.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:55.340
You'll be happy to know they deliver another
scathing indictment on capitalist culture…although

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.360
Velvet Buzzsaw is a far campier and cheekier
experience.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.360
"I think sober hasn't been good for him."

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.280
"Well this was done fifteen years ago.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.980
Piers was in the full bloom of alcoholism
here."

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.120
The film revolves around the fickle, opportunistic
world of modern art.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.460
We meet a vapid circle of taste-makers out
to get rich by selling off a recently unearthed

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.800
stack of paintings.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:17.700
The catch?

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.680
These paintings were created by a madman whose
spirit resides in each work of art…and now

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.920
the ghastly ghoul seeks revenge against anyone
who tries profiting from his work.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.580
Yes, the plot is as silly as it sounds.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.220
And yes, Gilroy fully embraces that silliness
throughout the film.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.020
He also tempers the mood with a heaping dose
of menace that transforms his parody into

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.340
a brooding, bloody beast of a film.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.560
Attention, body-horror fans…All body horror
fans, please report to Netflix immediately,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.760
and add Richard Shepard's The Perfection to
your queue immediately.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.700
Rest assured: even the biggest gorehounds
may get a little bit more than they bargained

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.600
for when they watch Shepard's harrowing psychological
thriller.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.300
At the center of this twisted tale are a pair
of cello prodigies, played with sinister zeal

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.600
and sensuality by Allison Williams and Logan
Browning.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.280
The opening moments of The Perfection find
the musicians at dramatically different points

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.120
in their lives.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.340
Williams' star has faded after she walked
away from music to care for her dying mother.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.400
Meanwhile, Browning's star shines bright within
the music world, particularly in the eyes

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.240
of the pair's haughty instructor.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.620
"I mean, I'm sorry. Am I the luckiest man in the world?

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.880
I don't even drink and I feel giddy drunk.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.640
My two most perfect students together."

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.340
When Charlotte finally finds herself free
of her familial obligations, she immediately

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.680
tries to work her way back into the music
world that she was forced to leave.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.120
An initial, seemingly mutual attraction between
the pair quickly puts both women on a road

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:43.910
trip...which rapidly devolves into a body
horror nightmare that would make David Cronenberg

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:44.400
cringe.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.320
And that's just the film's opening act.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.700
From there, well...things get a bit nuts,
with Shepard and crew twisting the unwieldy

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:53.520
narrative in increasingly unsettling ways.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.940
The Perfection proves itself to be a first-rate,
lavishly executed thriller…with a menacing

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.460
energy that's sure to satisfy the bloodthirsty
appetites of horror fans everywhere.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:09.280
By now, you're probably familiar with Jordan
Peele's pitch-perfect horror film Get Out.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.870
And if not, well...we urge you to go and watch
it immediately, because it's just as amazing

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:14.460
as you've heard.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.800
In fact, Peele's Oscar-winning debut was so
incredible, there were some very legitimate

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.760
concerns that he'd suffer the sophomore slump
that plagues so many other directors.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.740
Those worries only mounted when he announced
that he'd be returning to the horror genre

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:27.380
for his followup.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.560
Of course, all those concerns were quickly
laid to rest when Peele unleashed the bone-chilling

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.410
first trailer for Us.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.660
And five minutes into the director's latest
socially conscious fright-fest, you know you're

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.340
in for something wholly original, and wholly
horrific.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:45.819
Much like its predecessor, Us is a bold, incisive,
witty genre confection that happily wears

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.420
its influences on its sleeve.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:52.780
And yet somehow, it still doesn't look, feel,
or play like any horror film you've ever seen.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.240
As far as the plot goes, it's a relatively
simple tale that finds a family being terrorized

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.980
by evil versions of themselves.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.000
But much like Get Out, Peele has much bigger
fish to fry.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.900
Expect plenty of narrative twists and turns
along the way.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.860
"Who are you people?"

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.120
"It's us."

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.380
We won't spoil anything here…but you should
brace yourself for a near-suffocating sense

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:15.620
of existential dread.

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.620
And some serious bloodshed, too.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.860
With so many acclaimed genre offerings hitting
theaters, it's safe to say we're experiencing

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:24.600
a golden age of horror films.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.560
And since Stephen King is behind some of the
biggest hits in the bunch, it's also safe

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.480
to say we're living in a golden age of King
adaptations.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.860
While we anxiously wait for It: Chapter 2
to hit theaters, we can at least hunker down

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:41.529
and enjoy the grisly, emotionally punishing
new adaptation of King's best-selling novel,

00:04:41.529 --> 00:04:42.480
Pet Sematary.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.020
If you remember Mary Lambert's merciless 1989
adaptation, you're probably still suffering

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.360
from nightmares involving scalpel-wielding,
undead infants.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.740
The film offered a gritty form of horror that
made you want tp shower after the credits

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:55.480
rolled.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.419
If you're wary of the new Pet Sematary, be
warned that it's lost none of that grittiness…but

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:04.540
it features several important narrative changes
that are bound to disturb and disorient you

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.540
even more.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.860
The main thrust of the story remains the same.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.119
A family moves to Maine, tragic deaths ensue,
misguided resurrections follow, and undead

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.040
mayhem reigns.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.420
What sets this mayhem apart is that a child
happens to be behind much of the bloodletting.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.940
There's a relentless sense of atmospheric
anguish throughout Pet Sematary, and the film

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:25.560
really catches fire once the dead start to
rise.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.260
The film culminates in a horrifying, heartbreaking
finale that's bound to leave you gasping for

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:30.840
air.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.580
In the mood to be punished?

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.320
Have a look at Dragged Across Concrete starring
Vince Vaughn and Mel Gibson.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.120
"A single red ant could have eaten it faster."

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.219
The film stops just short of reaching over
and punching you in the face in its committed

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.740
attempt to shock, provoke, and condemn whoever
dares to sit through it.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.800
And the 159-minute runtime feels even longer
thanks to the film's slow pacing.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:01.360
Dragged Across Concrete takes a pulpy, ultra-violent
approach to cinema, depositing narcissistic,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.050
morally compromised tough guys into a brutal
and nightmarish world.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:09.900
Vaughn and Gibson's racist, easily corrupted
cops are the face of this particular nightmare.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.900
As such, the film never seeks to make heroes,
or even anti-heroes, out of either of them.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:20.020
The film presents an incurably ugly world
that many viewers will ultimately be uncomfortable

00:06:20.029 --> 00:06:23.789
with…but if you're looking for an exceedingly
disturbing night of entertainment, this film

00:06:23.789 --> 00:06:25.260
is definitely an option.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:31.080
Since its founding less than a decade ago,
A24 Films has turned into a bonafide indie

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:31.960
powerhouse.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:36.779
They've achieved this by producing a savvy
blend of stark, human dramas like Moonlight,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:41.889
Lady Bird, and Room, and artful genre fare
like Ex Machina, The Witch,

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:42.840
and Hereditary.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.940
This year saw A24 staying the course with
this release strategy, kicking off 2019 with

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.840
Lee Cronin's paranoid chiller The Hole in
the Ground.

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:55.080
If you're never heard of Cronin, it's because
The Hole in the Ground is the Irish filmmaker's

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.360
first feature film.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:00.900
Rest assured, it's a damn impressive debut,
one that finds a young mother and her son

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.560
attempting to build a new life for themselves
on the edges of a small rural town.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.940
Unfortunately, their new home exists on the
edge of a vast forest, and that forest contains

00:07:08.949 --> 00:07:14.029
an ominous, expansive sinkhole, which may
or may not be connected to the dramatic behavioral

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:15.260
changes in the boy.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.600
"Tell me the truth."

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.320
"I am."

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.740
"Tell me the truth!"

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:20.480
"I am!"

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:21.680
"Stop lying to me!"

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:24.380
"I'm not lying."

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.960
Turns out, they are indeed connected, and
that's not really a spoiler.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:32.060
For one thing, this plot point becomes obvious
early on in the film.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:36.380
Plus, The Hole in the Ground is less concerned
with shocking twists, and more intent on building

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.780
a bleak atmosphere full of fear and paranoia.

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:42.780
Cronin and company definitely succeed in that
regard, coupling their atmospheric chills

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:44.719
with a haunting, slow-burn approach.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.020
The film culminates in a finale that feels
like the stuff of Kafka's nightmares.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:54.880
Throughout his decades-long career behind
the camera, filmmaker Gaspar Noé has been

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.900
labeled everything from a shameless provocateur
to a cinematic visionary.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:02.980
As you may have guessed from those labels,
his films are just as likely to leave viewers

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.689
disgusted as they are to leave them utterly
entranced.

00:08:05.689 --> 00:08:09.529
Throughout his body of work, Noé doesn't
seem overly concerned with how viewers react

00:08:09.529 --> 00:08:12.620
to his movies so long as the films get some
sort of reaction.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:17.599
Following its 2018 international release and
later 2019 U.S. release, it became clear that

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:22.599
Climax marked another wild cinematic experiment
hellbent on pushing the boundaries of both

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.940
narrative decency and artistic ambition.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:30.280
To be clear, Climax is a technical, artistic,
and narrative achievement as potent as anything

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:31.720
Noé has ever created before.

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:36.580
It's also certain to force many viewers to
run far away at the first sign of the madness

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:37.460
to come.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:42.240
The real lunacy arrives about midway through
Climax…right around the time the dancing

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:45.460
troupe at the heart of the film discovers
that their beverages have been spiked with

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:46.720
a powerful narcotic.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.640
That's about as much plot as we're comfortable
giving away...not because we're scared of

00:08:50.641 --> 00:08:54.660
spoiling anything, but because words simply
can't describe the hallucinatory hellscape

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:56.600
that Noé unleashes upon you.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:10.760
Just know that it's often difficult to watch
the insanity unfold…and it's equally difficult

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:11.720
to look away.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:18.800
Actor Robert Pattinson really outdoes himself
in High Life, portraying a man desperately

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.620
trying to survive alongside his daughter,
while both of them are lost deep in space.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:27.520
Believe us when we say this film puts the
"psycho" back in "psychosexual."

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.680
For those wondering how Pattinson's character
wound up in the outer reaches of the galaxy,

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.240
it's because he was a very bad man on planet
Earth.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:38.960
Along with several other offenders, he signed
up for a radical reproductive experiment instead

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:39.900
of dying in prison.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:43.120
Let it suffice to say, that may have been
the wrong choice.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.550
Sound weird enough for you?

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.110
Well, that's just the tip of the iceberg.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:49.960
This is a fantastically twisted movie, and
at the center of all the insanity is one of

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.560
the best performances of Pattinson's career.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.680
The gripping western-horror film The Wind
finds plenty of genuine chills within its

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.060
hushed, haunted narrative.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:03.520
The film revolves around a homesteader left
alone amidst a sprawling prairie landscape

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.460
in the wake of an unspeakable tragedy.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.140
The culmination of that tragedy plays a vital
part in the film's overarching narrative,

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:14.020
and The Wind is at its most effective when
the woman is trapped in total seclusion.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:15.860
"The lord is my keeper…"

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.500
This is an almost perfect exercise in psychological
horror.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.820
In fact, the film is so effective, you're
willing to overlook the fact that certain

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.320
elements of the dialogue and storyline leaves
something to be desired.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:38.700
Hagazussa is so slow-paced, it's bound to
test the mettle of even the fiercest horror

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:39.220
fan.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:42.800
But if you stick with the film, you'll eventually
arrive at one of the most gut-wrenchingly

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.660
horrific finales in recent memory.

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:49.640
Set almost entirely in the mountainous terrain
of 15th-century Europe, Hagazussa follows

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:53.970
an outcast struggling to maintain her sanity
while living in almost total isolation with

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:54.960
her newborn daughter.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.840
The film's pacing effectively lulls the viewer
into a trance, making those bone-chilling

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.160
final moments all the more devastating.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.840
It's totally okay if you've never heard of
Possum.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:09.740
The film never got a theatrical release stateside,
and after a well-received yet limited run

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:14.850
in its native U.K. in 2018, it went straight
to VOD for U.S. markets earlier this year.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:19.110
And after you watch this emotionally grueling
film, you'll see why the majority of viewers

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.320
will want to stay away.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:21.540
But not you, right?

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:22.820
You're not afraid of anything...

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:26.620
A puppet features prominently in Possum, but
not just any puppet.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:31.000
This one is shaped like a giant spider with
a monstrous human head attached…and it looks

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:32.760
a little something like this!

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:45.520
Coupled with the film's suffocating sense
of dread, Possum is definitely an exceedingly

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:46.740
difficult film to watch.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:51.220
Brace yourself for a harrowing tale of a man
who's damaged beyond all repair by childhood

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:52.160
abuse and trauma.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:57.431
The film is beautifully photographed and skillfully
acted…but it's so relentlessly bleak, it

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:01.040
makes David Lynch's Eraserhead look like a
walk in the park.

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.580
Consider yourself warned on this one, okay?

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.580
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:17.460
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
films are coming soon.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:21.820
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

